WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:02.455
Last lesson's challenge was to make print

2
00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:03.556
say your own name.

3
00:00:04.298 --> 00:00:06.261
If you wrote your name straight inside

4
00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:08.804
print, you had to repeat it every time.

5
00:00:08.844 --> 00:00:11.809
But in the final script, you probably had

6
00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:12.710
a line like this:

7
00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:14.814
name = "Ada".

8
00:00:15.395 --> 00:00:16.937
That line is a variable.

9
00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:19.982
This lesson is all about what that line

10
00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:20.743
really does.

11
00:00:21.584 --> 00:00:23.928
A variable is how a program remembers a

12
00:00:23.988 --> 00:00:26.832
value, so it can use it again later.

13
00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:30.060
When you write name = "Ada", you're

14
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.203
storing the value "Ada" under the name

15
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:32.563
name.

16
00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:36.027
Now, the equals sign here means "store

17
00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:38.390
this", not "equals" like in math.

18
00:00:39.051 --> 00:00:40.392
You put the name of the box on

19
00:00:40.413 --> 00:00:42.355
the left, and the value on the right.

20
00:00:43.016 --> 00:00:45.318
After that, whenever you write name, it

21
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.161
means whatever is in that box.

22
00:00:47.861 --> 00:00:49.984
Let's look at that in a picture.

23
00:00:49.984 --> 00:00:52.371
Here’s a diagram showing how it works.

24
00:00:53.013 --> 00:00:55.159
You see that the label stays, but the

25
00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:56.463
contents can change.

26
00:00:57.385 --> 00:00:59.571
So when you run this code, it prints

27
00:00:59.612 --> 00:01:02.299
Ada twice because it looks inside the box

28
00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:03.282
named name.

29
00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.047
When you write print("name"),

30
00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:07.571
it shows the text name.

31
00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:09.093
But if you write print(name),

32
00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:11.818
it shows what the variable holds.

33
00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:14.823
Quotes mean "these exact characters",

34
00:01:15.223 --> 00:01:17.126
while no quotes mean "look inside the

35
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:17.547
box".

36
00:01:18.489 --> 00:01:20.752
So if you run this code, you'll see

37
00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:22.696
that the first print gives you the word

38
00:01:22.776 --> 00:01:25.040
name, and the second one gives you the

39
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:25.921
value Ada.

40
00:01:26.802 --> 00:01:29.607
It’s a simple but important distinction.

41
00:01:30.143 --> 00:01:32.245
When choosing names for your variables,

42
00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:35.107
remember that legal names can use letters,

43
00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:38.090
digits, and underscores, but they can't

44
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.011
start with a digit.

45
00:01:39.912 --> 00:01:43.735
Also, Python is case-sensitive, so age and

46
00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:45.517
Age are two different boxes.

47
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:48.399
It's best to choose names that describe

48
00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:49.280
what's inside.

49
00:01:49.981 --> 00:01:52.283
For example, birth_year is clearer than

50
00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:52.863
just by.

51
00:01:53.684 --> 00:01:56.226
And if you have multi-word names, use

52
00:01:56.327 --> 00:01:58.909
underscores, like favorite_color instead

53
00:01:58.949 --> 00:01:59.650
of favoritecolor.

54
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.754
Assigning again replaces the old value.

55
00:02:04.395 --> 00:02:07.017
The box keeps its label, but the contents

56
00:02:07.077 --> 00:02:07.718
are swapped.

57
00:02:08.359 --> 00:02:11.322
The old value is gone, and Python only

58
00:02:11.382 --> 00:02:13.144
remembers the latest assignment.

59
00:02:13.825 --> 00:02:15.226
Let's look at that in a picture.

60
00:02:16.127 --> 00:02:18.370
This diagram shows how assigning again

61
00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.051
replaces what was inside.

62
00:02:20.852 --> 00:02:22.954
When you run this code, you’ll see that

63
00:02:22.974 --> 00:02:25.777
the first print shows 0, and after we

64
00:02:25.838 --> 00:02:28.180
assign again, it shows 10.

65
00:02:28.987 --> 00:02:31.650
Everything from last lesson's math works

66
00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:32.811
inside variables.

67
00:02:33.592 --> 00:02:35.495
You can even use a variable's current

68
00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:37.056
value to compute a new one.

69
00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:40.500
For example, if you start with apples =

70
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.965
3 and then write apples = apples +

71
00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:46.928
2, you’re adding 2 to the current value.

72
00:02:47.469 --> 00:02:49.831
When you print apples, it gives you 5.

73
00:02:50.592 --> 00:02:52.294
It’s like counting your apples and

74
00:02:52.374 --> 00:02:53.876
finding out you have more than you

75
00:02:53.936 --> 00:02:54.657
thought!

76
00:02:55.312 --> 00:02:58.197
In real programs, you often mix several

77
00:02:58.237 --> 00:02:59.579
variables in one line.

78
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.423
The print function can take multiple

79
00:03:02.504 --> 00:03:05.448
things separated by commas, and it prints

80
00:03:05.508 --> 00:03:07.271
them with spaces in between.

81
00:03:07.912 --> 00:03:10.857
For instance, if you have name = "Ada"

82
00:03:10.897 --> 00:03:13.922
and age = 12, you can print them

83
00:03:13.982 --> 00:03:14.323
together.

84
00:03:15.244 --> 00:03:17.748
This prints a nice sentence about Ada and

85
00:03:17.808 --> 00:03:18.309
her age.

86
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.953
It’s a great way to share information in

87
00:03:20.993 --> 00:03:22.095
a clear way.

88
00:03:22.818 --> 00:03:25.003
In the next lesson, we’ll dive into text

89
00:03:25.083 --> 00:03:27.508
itself and explore what strings can do,

90
00:03:27.548 --> 00:03:29.772
from joining to changing case.

91
00:03:30.634 --> 00:03:32.879
But before then, here’s a challenge for

92
00:03:32.919 --> 00:03:35.745
you: create a script with two variables,

93
00:03:36.025 --> 00:03:38.170
your name and your favorite number, and

94
00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.994
print one sentence using both.

95
00:03:40.635 --> 00:03:42.419
It’s a fun way to practice what you’ve

96
00:03:42.479 --> 00:03:43.020
learned!

